{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1238359-a-question-for-storytellers-who-ve-ran-games-with-their-players-for-years","id":1238359,"title":"A question for Storytellers who've ran games with their players for years.","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2018-07-24T14:01:23-07:00","id":1238359,"title":"A question for Storytellers who've ran games with their players for years.","content":"How do you keep the games interesting and feeling fresh?<br /> <br /> I've both given and played in games I had with my best friend for years, and the World of Darkness (mainly old world and new world (as far as 1e though)) is the main source of our entertainment when it comes to games. I've given loads of games to him when starting out, but after hitting a Great Wall of Storyteller's Block I wasn't able to give sessions as much as I could and started actually playing one for once. This continued for a few years or so, but as of late, he's been lacking in inspiration for STing. <br /> <br /> So far he reasons it as, since we know each other so well, that I'm getting predictable. We've tried giving it another go, I'm trying to play in a different way but he's still borking out. I've tried offering giving sessions again, but he rejects based on similar premises. <br /> <br /> He's also reasoned that playing including other people nullifies the &quot;predictability&quot; issue, but as of yet we haven't tried such a game.<br /> <br /> So it all wraps back to the first question. Does anyone, player or storyteller, have any tips or helpful thoughts that might aid in this? I don't want to lose one of the biggest sources of fun I've had with my best friend.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Currently Doing: Siren the Drowning (Fansplat Ressurection) <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/779860-siren-the-surfacing-nwod-abandoned-fangame-resurrection\" target=\"_blank\">http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/m...e-resurrection</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9750-saulotthegentle","name":"SaulottheGentle","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9750_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"453"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-24T14:24:40-07:00","id":1238368,"title":"","content":"Here are some options that have worked for my ST and I, who have been taking turns running games for one another for something like 15 years now:<br /> <br /> 1. Choose a distinct theme when starting a new game. Different themes will lead to different stories. <br /> 2. Don't be afraid to steal ideas. If you read a lot of books, you will be exposed to a lots of ideas. Use them shamelessly. <br /> 3. Character driven plots. I'm not just talking about PC driven. I mean spend some time on the head space of major NPC's in your setting. You may think of things while in those head spaces you would ordinarily not and lead yourself to interesting ideas.<br /> 4. I have in the past has brain storming sessions with friends of mine who are into running/playing rpgs, but who are not involved in the game I am running. <br /> 5. Invite other people to play. Whether permanently or temporarily, the actions of another PC can act as a randomizer.<br /> <br /> Cheers","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>There Are Four Lights!<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/92949-baltimore-the-kiddie-pool-saga-pics-maps-and-links\" target=\"_blank\">[Actual Play] Baltimore: The Kiddie Pool Saga</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/64-figurehead","name":"figurehead","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar64_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"203"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-24T16:01:50-07:00","id":1238402,"title":"","content":"I\u2019ve got to agree with figurehead. Those are some quality suggestions. I would like to add my own thoughts, too. I play regularly with my wife, almost daily if nothing else is going on. Not every session is going to be a winner, but you get a good one, it\u2019s worth it. So here\u2019s a couple thoughts:<br /> 1. Design basic story ideas for each other, with some thoughts on what you\u2019d like as a player. Yes, I\u2019m suggesting that you let your friend design a story he\u2019d like to play that you would then run. Once you\u2019ve got that basic idea, put your own spin on it and run with it.<br /> 2. Interdimensional travel. Every new reality resets expectations. I\u2019ve been running a game centered around this idea for over two years now for my wife\u2019s omnipotent character, with reasonably good results. It started out as an Exalted/Changeling: The Lost mashup, and now her character has wandered through damn near every game world we\u2019ve got access to, plus plenty of pop culture references and bizarre original worlds. Currently, she\u2019s acquired an entourage of friendly animal people in Pugmire. If you take your friend\u2019s character from one reality to another, you can pull off all sorts of bizarre crap, and leave any problems behind when they go to the next one. Hell, you could alternate who is the player with each world if you liked. Mechanically, just approximate as best you can with your central system, or go diceless (which is what we did).<br /> 3. Play a series of \u201cone-shot\u201d games using different games for each one. Gradually reveal that all of them were connected, and that it was one big game with many perspectives. <br /> 4. Try vastly different game systems and settings. The further removed you get from the familiar, the fresher it should seem when you come back to the ones you know and love.<br /> 5. Play a joke game. I\u2019m serious. Find something goofy (like Paranoia or Kobolds Ate My Baby) or run one you\u2019ve got in a goofy way. The important thing is to keep the laughs coming.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Jason Ross Inczauskis</b>, Freelance Writer<br /> Projects: <i>Dark Eras 2</i>, <i>Mummy: The Curse 2e, Book of Lasting Death, DtR The Clades Companion, Pirates of Pugmire,</i> <i>They Came From Beyond the Grave!, </i><i>TC Aeon: Mission Statements</i>, <i>TC In Media Res,</i> <i>DtD Night Horrors: Enemy Action,</i> <i>C20 Anthology of Dreams</i><br /> Masculine pronouns preferred.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/309-white-oak-dragon","name":"White Oak Dragon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"941"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-24T22:47:47-07:00","id":1238519,"title":"","content":"If your friend's primary complaint is that you as a player have become predictable then I recommend playing a game where you don't create a character, but steal one.<br /> Pick a character out of a book or TV show, just as long as it goes long enough to portray the character fully, write up that character, apply game template and then do your best to play them to character. You may be a paranoid and over planner but Laura Croft tends to jump in head first and scramble to survive, while Reddington never goes in blind if he can help it and seeks to manipulate everything around him.<br /> Not only does this have the potential to make your play less predictable but attempting to play the role can be challenging and fun in its own right.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6084-2pttakrill","name":"2ptTakrill","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"942"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-24T23:10:36-07:00","id":1238522,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SaulottheGentle</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1238359-a-question-for-storytellers-who-ve-ran-games-with-their-players-for-years#post1238359\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So it all wraps back to the first question. Does anyone, player or storyteller, have any tips or helpful thoughts that might aid in this? I don't want to lose one of the biggest sources of fun I've had with my best friend.</div> </div> </div> </div>Everyone's got good ideas.<br /> <br /> Stealing is good, especially if it's from something neither of you have been exposed much to or know about. If you're close friends with shared hobbies, you've likely taken in similar media, so going out of your normal safety zone can be good for coming up with new ideas. Playing other games can help in a big way, Chronicles has a lot of different themes across the different gamelines, and with Dark Eras you have things like Neolithic Mage and Werewolf, which play things drastically differently (you can potentially be playing in a time where the wheel wasn't invented yet). We haven't had the same problem (thankfully) but after playing a Mage game that had some Neolithic chapters (they really piqued the players interests), we're now playing Werewolf in a more straightforwardly fantasy setting. Stealing is just another form of shaking things up, getting some outside influences in your games.<br /> <br /> Storyteller burnout is totally a thing, too. You might try and give him a break and run again.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}